How, exactly, did you do this?  root can do pretty much anything. 
Giving other users executable permission on your home directory allows
them to get in, but access to files is determined by the permission on
the individual files.  Giving world executable to $HOME, and world
readable to .nethackrc would solve the problem.

Another option is to set the environment variable NETHACKOPTIONS with
the value of the full filename you want to use as a config file:

export NETHACKOPTIONS=/usr/games/.nethackrc
setenv NETHACKOPTIONS /usr/games/.nethackrc

where the former is appropriate for bash, the latter for (t)csh.  Put
this in a startup file to make sure it's set for all sessions:

~/.profile for you, if using bash
/etc/profile for all users using bash
~/.cshrc for you, if using (t)csh
/etc/csh.login for all users using (t)csh

